You can use a union type to specify the multiple types that a value may have in theory, and then add an assertion to the variable declaration to specify the actual type that the value will have in practice. For example: 
```typescript
type User = {
  name: string;
  age?: number;
}

// This allows `user` to have either a string or a number value
let user: User | number;

// This asserts that `user` will always have a string value
user = 'Bob' as User;
// user.age is now defined as a number, because of the type assertion
console.log(user.age); // undefinedassistantlásil

In this example, `user` is declared as a variable of type `User` or `number`. The union type allows the value to have either type, but the type assertion ensures that it will always be of type `User`. 
This is useful when you know that in theory a value may have multiple types, but in practice you know it will only have one specific type. It can prevent errors and improve code clarity by making it clear to the compiler and other developers which type the variable is expected to have.assistant

